    <body>
      <sec id="article-26374.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Primary osteoarthritis, the most prevalent form of osteoarthritis, represents a progressive, age-associated condition characterized by cartilage degeneration, joint space narrowing, and functional impairment. This continuing education activity provides a comprehensive review of its pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and evidence-based management strategies, emphasizing recognition of mechanical joint pain and common involvement of the hands, knees, and hips. Participants gain insight into both nonpharmacologic and pharmacologic treatment approaches, including lifestyle modification and symptom-directed therapies. Emphasis is placed on early identification and individualized care planning to reduce disability and improve quality of life. Collaboration among an interprofessional team, including clinicians, physical therapists, and specialists, enhances care coordination, optimizes treatment adherence, and leads to improved patient-centered outcomes in the management of primary osteoarthritis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Apply advanced evidence-based strategies in the management of primary osteoarthritis, incorporating patient comorbidities, preferences, and risk stratification to optimize outcomes.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Evaluate longitudinal treatment effectiveness using patient-reported outcomes, functional assessments, and quality-of-life measures.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate primary osteoarthritis from inflammatory arthritides and other musculoskeletal conditions using clinical, laboratory, and imaging criteria.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Compare&#x000a0;collaborative team strategies for improving care coordination and communication to enhance outcomes and advance the care of primary osteoarthritis.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=26374&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=26374">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Primary osteoarthritis represents the most common form of arthritis encountered in clinical practice and remains a leading cause of disability worldwide. Radiographic evidence of disease is present in the majority of individuals by age 65 and is observed in approximately 80% of those older than 75, underscoring its strong association with aging and its substantial impact on population health. Osteoarthritis can range from an asymptomatic condition to debilitating oligo or polyarthritis. Mechanical pain (pain with activity) is the most common presenting symptom of the disease. The hands, knees, and hips are the most commonly affected joints, although any joint can be affected.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r2">[2]</xref>&#x000a0;This article will discuss primary osteoarthritis, where no specific etiology has been identified.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The etiology of primary osteoarthritis remains incompletely understood and is thought to involve a complex interplay of genetic and environmental factors. Multiple gene regions, including <italic toggle="yes">IL1</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">FRZB</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">MATN3</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">BMP5</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">LRP5</italic>, and <italic toggle="yes">IL4R</italic>, have been associated with disease susceptibility, along with other candidate genes&#x000a0;(see&#x000a0;<bold>Table 1</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r4">[4]</xref> However, these genetic associations are generally modest and lack consistent strength, highlighting the multifactorial nature of disease development and the limited predictive value of individual genetic markers.</p>
        <p>Epigenetic mechanisms are estimated to contribute approximately 30% to 65% to the development of primary osteoarthritis, underscoring the importance of gene&#x02013;environment interactions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;&#x000a0;Hemochromatosis is associated with an atypical osteoarthritis distribution, commonly affecting the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints, and confers an increased risk for joint replacement surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r7">[7]</xref>&#x000a0;The <italic toggle="yes">HFE</italic> gene mutation p.C282Y has been linked to a higher risk of osteoarthritis, particularly involving the hands.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r9">[9]</xref> Prior joint injury, especially involving the knee, such as meniscal damage or repair, is a well-established risk factor that significantly increases the likelihood of developing osteoarthritis over time.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Age is the strongest risk factor for osteoarthritis; this condition may be linked to decreased joint adaptability to resist biomechanical stress, sarcopenia, and increased bone turnover. Female sex is associated with a higher prevalence of osteoarthritis due to differences in joint alignment, ligament strength, the effect of pregnancy, and lower bone density between men and women. Menopause, in particular, is associated with an increased risk of osteoarthritis, suggesting the role of estrogen in&#x000a0;osteoarthritis development and pain sensitization. Anatomical factors, including joint malalignment or congenital deformities, can lead to biomechanical injury, increasing the susceptibility for developing secondary osteoarthritis. Modifiable environmental factors play a major role in the development of&#x000a0;osteoarthritis; this includes occupations requiring repetitive movements, obesity, smoking, muscle weakness, and low bone density.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>Obesity is linked to osteoarthritis, especially in the knee. The risk of developing knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis is associated with increasing body mass index (BMI) and an increased risk of total knee replacement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;In a systematic review, weight gain was associated with increased severity of knee osteoarthritis, including symptoms of pain, radiographic changes on plain radiographs and MRI, and rates of total knee replacement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;In a large population-based cohort study of 1,764,061 individuals, overweight and obesity were associated not only with knee involvement but also with increased risk of hip and hand manifestations of osteoarthritis. In patients with a BMI of 35 kg/m<sup>2</sup>&#x000a0;or greater, the adjusted hazard ratios were 4.72 (95% CI 4.56&#x02013;4.89) for knee osteoarthritis, 1.93 (95% CI 1.82-2.05) for hip osteoarthritis, and 1.31 (95% CI 1.24-1.38) for hand osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>Other risk factors for&#x000a0;osteoarthritis include physically demanding work and physical activity. Occupations that are physically demanding have been associated with knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis (odds ratio 18.3, 95% CI 4.2-79.4) and hip&#x000a0;osteoarthritis (hazard ratios for males 12.7, females 15.2).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;In general, routine recreational exercise has not been associated with osteoarthritis, but high levels of physical activity&#x000a0;are modestly associated with knee but not hip osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Quadriceps weakness and dysfunction have been recognized to be associated with knee osteoarthritis, even in patients with asymptomatic disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;Quadriceps weakness is associated with the radiographic progression of knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in all patients. However, one study found this was only in women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Vitamin D status has also been associated with osteoarthritis. In a longitudinal study of 353 patients evaluated at baseline and again after 3 years, lower sun exposure and serum 25(OH) vitamin D levels below 50 nmol/L (20 ng/mL) were associated with both the presence of knee osteoarthritis and disease progression, as assessed by cartilage volume on MRI and joint space narrowing on radiographs. This suggests that vitamin D deficiency is associated with the presence and progression of knee osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;In a study of 1248 patients followed prospectively for 6 years, low dietary vitamin D intake and vitamin D levels were not associated with the prevalence of knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis at baseline but with an increased risk of progression of knee osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r25">[25]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>A study of 1785 patients seen at baseline and then at 4 years showed that vitamin D supplementation was associated with less progression of knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis as measured by plain radiographs and MRI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;However, one study's results using&#x000a0;National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey data showed that a vitamin D level of less than 20 mg/mL was associated with a lower risk of&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in men (OR 0.35, 95% CI 0.15&#x02013;0.81,&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">P&#x000a0;</italic>= 0.02) but not in women.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Crystal diseases have been suggested as potential etiological factors in osteoarthritis, but there is only circumstantial evidence, particularly calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate crystals (chondrocalcinosis is associated with OA).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r28">[28]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
