</td>
                <td style="width: 363px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Vitamin D receptor</td>
                <td style="width: 847.438px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Nuclear receptor: mediates the effects of Vitamin D; low levels affect the incidence and severity of osteoarthritis</td>
                <td style="width: 551.562px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r3">[3]</xref></td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Osteoarthritis is the most prevalent rheumatic disease and a leading cause of disability worldwide. Joint replacement surgeries represent the most frequently performed orthopedic procedures in many countries. Osteoarthritis most commonly affects the knees, hands, hips, and feet. Degenerative disc disease of the lumbar and cervical spine is often associated with osteoarthritis of the facet joints.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Primary osteoarthritis does not typically affect the shoulders, wrists,&#x000a0;metacarpophalangeal joints, and ankles, although these joints may be involved in secondary osteoarthritis. Osteoarthritis is uncommon in individuals&#x000a0;younger than 40, is higher in women, and increases as the population ages, leveling off around age 75.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;<bold>Table 2</bold> shows the prevalence of radiographic and symptomatic&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in different regions worldwide.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref> The prevalence worldwide indicates that&#x000a0;osteoarthritis is more common in the United States, Central Asia, and Subsaharan Africa, followed by Central Asia, China, and Central and South America.&#x000a0;The prevalence is higher in women and peaks at age 60 in about 25% in women and 16% in men.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r1">[1]</xref></p>
        <p>The prevalence of radiographic knee osteoarthritis in population-based studies was 19.2% among adults older than 45 in the Framingham Heart Study and 37% among adults older than 60 in the Third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES III).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;An analysis of patients in southern Sweden reported that physician-diagnosed osteoarthritis affecting any joint had a prevalence of 26.6% among individuals older than 45.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref> In Finland, the prevalence of hip and knee osteoarthritis was 5.6%. The prevalence was higher in women and those aged 60 to 65. In women aged 60 to 65, hip osteoarthritis was present in 2.3% and knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in 12.8%, and in men, hip&#x000a0;osteoarthritis was present in 2.2% and knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in 10.9%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r32">[32]</xref></p>
