                <td style="width: 291.594px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">22%&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;2%&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; 45%</td>
                <td style="width: 300.406px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">15%&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; -&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;6%</td>
                <td style="width: 306px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;6%&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;2%&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0;3%</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>When examining hand osteoarthritis specifically, prevalence was higher in women than in men and increased with age. Both radiographic and symptomatic osteoarthritis were more common in White individuals compared with Black individuals (see&#x000a0;<bold>Table 3</bold>).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;A pattern of&#x000a0;osteoarthritis called erosive (or inflammatory)&#x000a0;osteoarthritis typically involves the proximal interphalangeal and&#x000a0;distal interphalangeal joints, and radiographs show central erosion developed in 2.6% of patients with hand osteoarthritis over 4 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
