          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>Osteoarthritis is associated with several medical comorbidities, including obesity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r36">[36]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Obesity was associated with a 3-fold increased risk of this condition. Osteoarthritis is also associated with metabolic syndrome, including hypertension, cardiovascular disease, diabetes mellitus, and hyperlipidemia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Knee and hip osteoarthritis is associated with an increase in all-cause mortality, which may be related to the association of osteoarthritis with obesity and elements of the metabolic syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r41">[41]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathogenesis of primary osteoarthritis is complex&#x000a0;and involves mechanical, metabolic, and inflammatory factors, resulting in an imbalance between repair and destruction of joint tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;This is now understood to be more than simply a wear-and-tear disease as historically described and involves not just the cartilage but a failure of the entire joint, including the cartilage, subchondral bone, synovial membrane, and the joint capsule with ligaments and tendons. This results in cartilage erosions, subchondral bone changes, synovial inflammation, angiogenesis, and joint capsule ligaments and tendons instability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r42">[42]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>In the early stages,&#x000a0;cartilage composition changes, resulting in surface fibrillations that allow increased water content, which alters its resistance to biomechanical stress.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r43">[43]</xref> This leads to&#x000a0;erosions and&#x000a0;later deeper cartilaginous fissures. The stress on cartilage promotes chondrocyte proliferation and activation in an attempt to repair, producing proteoglycans and matrix-degrading enzymes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>Later in the disease, chondrocytes undergo apoptosis, decreasing the number of cells. With cartilage degradation, the subchondral bone becomes denser, and osteophytes form. There is low-grade inflammation in the synovium with the production of proinflammatory cytokines, including interleukin (IL)-1B, IL-6, IL-15, IL-17, IL-18, and tumor necrosis-&#x003b1;, among others.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r43">[43]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>On the anatomic level, the earliest process in&#x000a0;osteoarthritis is articular cartilage damage, including surface fibrillation, irregularity, and focal erosions. Cartilage damage causes chondrocyte proliferation, and outgrowths can ossify and form osteophytes or bone spurs. Subchondral bone sclerosis and bone cyst formation occur later in the disease and may increase joint stiffness and pain; advanced disease results in episodic synovitis. Although rare, bony erosions may occur in erosive osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref></p>
        <p>Various intracellular molecules and metalloproteases play a role in the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis. Wnt signaling is important in normal cartilage growth and homeostasis, and in osteoarthritis, cartilage is associated with the production of proteases such as matrix metalloproteinases and aggrecanases.&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">Runx1</italic>, a nuclear transcription factor, may play a protective role in osteoarthritis by inhibiting Wnt signaling.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r45">[45]</xref> This includes matrix metalloproteinases 1, 3, 9, and 13 (MMP-1, MMP-3, MMP-9, and MMP-13), aggrecanases, a disintegrin and metalloproteinase with thrombospondin motifs 4 and 5 (ADAMTS4 and ADAMTS5), and cathepsin K. These proteases lead to increased cartilage destruction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r30">[30]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>MMPs, including MMP13 and ADAMTS4 and 5, are expressed in osteoarthritic cartilage. This results in accelerated cartilage matrix destruction. Some of the molecules are of interest as therapeutic targets.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r47">[47]</xref>&#x000a0;Transforming growth factor-B3 is an important cytokine for normal cartilage growth and homeostasis, but in osteoarthritic cartilage, high levels induce the production of proteases and apoptosis of chondrocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r48">[48]</xref>&#x000a0;In normal cartilage, there is a balance between apoptosis (programmed cell death) and autophagy (handling and removing damaged cellular debris and macromolecules), which is necessary for normal cartilage growth and homeostasis. In osteoarthritic cartilage, these processes become imbalanced with decreased autophagy and increased apoptosis, resulting in deterioration.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r50">[50]</xref></p>
        <p>Monocytes&#x000a0;are found in osteoarthritic synovium, including&#x000a0;CD14<sup>+</sup>CD16<sup>-</sup> monocytes. CD14<sup>++</sup>CD16<sup>-</sup> monocytes are abundant in the osteoarthritic synovial tissue and have an activated phenotype with the expression of several receptors, proinflammatory intracellular proteins, and cytokines, including IL-1B and tumor necrosis factor a.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r51">[51]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r52">[52]</xref>&#x000a0;CD14<sup>+</sup>CD16<sup>+</sup> monocytes are enriched in osteoarthritic synovium compared to CD14<sup>+</sup>CD16<sup>-</sup> monocytes. CD14 acts as a coreceptor for toll-like receptor 2 and 4, helping to trigger the inflammatory cascade. Soluble CD14, combined with LPs, stimulated the production of IL-6, IL-8, and MMP3.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r52">[52]</xref></p>
        <p>Proinflammatory cytokines appear to have a role in the pathogenesis of osteoarthritis. Some cytokines directly affect cartilage damage: IL-6, IL-17, IL-22, and IL-33. Some cytokines cause damage indirectly by stimulating the production of other proinflammatory cytokines (IL-1, IL-6, IL-7, IL-17, and IL-34) or&#x000a0;proteases (IL-15).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r53">[53]</xref>&#x000a0;The inflammatory response is important in&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in terms of the progression of the joint damage and also producing pain, but this is thought to be secondary to the structural and mechanical factors and proteolytic enzymes, but it plays a role in the pain.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>In a healthy synovial joint, bone ends are covered by an elastic layer of articular hyaline cartilage. This layer mainly consists of water, type II collagen that maintains the structure, and proteoglycans that provide the cartilage with the ability to withstand compressive and shear forces. Early in osteoarthritis, loss of proteoglycans disrupts the cartilage fibers, which leads to cartilage softening and increases the water content. The earliest sign of cartilage damage observed by light microscopy is chondral fibrillation, followed by cracks and deeper fissures.</p>
        <p>Cartilage loss promotes chondrocyte proliferation and activation. In type I collagen, a fibroblast&#x000a0;matrix fills&#x000a0;the areas that have lost cartilage and is called a &#x0201c;pannus.&#x0201d; The remodeled matrix can then become calcified and invaded by small vessels later in the disease. In end-stage disease, the subchondral bone becomes thicker and sclerotic. The margins of the articular bone form outgrowths called &#x0201c;bone spurs&#x0201d; or &#x0201c;osteophytes.&#x0201d; Bone cysts may appear connected to the subchondral surface by a fissure filled with inflammatory fluid, myxoid material, or bone fragments.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r54">[54]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The diagnosis of osteoarthritis is primarily clinical. Primary&#x000a0;osteoarthritis is a heterogeneous disease with different phenotypes apparent after a history and physical exam. Primary osteoarthritis is usually a symmetric disease, but patients may have dominant disease in certain joints such as the knee, hip, or hand.</p>
        <p>Classically, osteoarthritis&#x000a0;presents with joint pain that is worse with use and improves with rest. The pain usually peaks in the late afternoon or early evening and can also be evident in the early morning upon awakening from sleep. The symptoms progress over several years, and radiographic evidence of&#x000a0;osteoarthritis almost always predates the symptoms. Over time, the pain becomes more severe at rest and even during sleep. Spinal involvement in&#x000a0;osteoarthritis occurs due to disc disease and may be associated with radicular manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>Pain can be divided into&#x000a0;2 types:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Dull, aching, throbbing pain that becomes more constant over time and is predictable. Pain can also be accompanied by stiffness after prolonged immobilization (also known as the "gelling phenomenon").</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Intense, unpredictable pain for short periods. This can be emotionally traumatizing and limit the patient's readiness to participate in daily activities.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Based on the aforementioned types of pain, osteoarthritis can be clinically classified into&#x000a0;3 stages:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Early osteoarthritis: There is predictable, sharp pain often triggered by a mechanical stimulus that limits the patient's ability to participate in high-impact activities.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Mid osteoarthritis: In addition to the pain described in early osteoarthritis, there is unpredictable joint pain or locking,&#x000a0;particularly in the knee. The pain becomes increasingly more constant and can affect daily activities.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Advanced osteoarthritis: Constant dull-aching pain with intermittent short episodes of unpredictable intense pain that results in avoidance of engaging in recreational or social activities. The pain now occurs at rest and during the night. There may also be prominent morning stiffness.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Additional joint symptoms include tenderness, stiffness, crepitus, limited range of movement, joint swelling, deformity, or instability. Instability is typically found with knee osteoarthritis, where patients lose their balance when the knee "gives out."&#x000a0;Findings on physical examination in&#x000a0;osteoarthritis include bony enlargement (osteophytes) (most commonly affecting the&#x000a0;proximal interphalangeal (PIP) and distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the fingers and toes), crepitus&#x02014;especially in the knees, effusions (noninflammatory), joint line tenderness, and limited range of motion due to pain, swelling, or joint deformity.</p>
        <p>Heberden nodes describe the posterolateral bony swelling of the DIP joints, and Bouchard nodes describe the posterolateral bony swelling of the PIP joints. Osteoarthritis involving the base of the thumb (first carpometacarpal joint) is often described as having a "shoulder" or "squaring" appearance. Deformities may occur in the hands, including flexion and angular deformities of the PIP and DIP joints, valgus, varus, or flexion deformities in the knee, and hallux valgus in the feet.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r56">[56]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r57">[57]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r58">[58]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r59">[59]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r60">[60]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>No laboratory test can diagnose osteoarthritis, as the diagnosis is clinically based. Imaging can aid in diagnosing osteoarthritis and can be used to exclude other types of arthritis. The alkaline phosphatase may be mildly elevated due to new bone formation in the form of osteophytes. Inflammatory markers, including the erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein, are usually normal in osteoarthritis but may help rule out other conditions when the diagnosis is uncertain. Additional laboratory testing can exclude inflammatory conditions such as rheumatoid arthritis, which may include a rheumatoid factor and anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies. Genetic&#x000a0;research is underway into aberrations in collagen formation and other collagen matrix proteins, which lead to premature osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r55">[55]</xref></p>
        <p>Various imaging modalities are available to confirm the diagnosis and evaluate the severity of osteoarthritis. The most commonly used modality in clinical practice is plain radiography. The European League Against Rheumatism recommends plain radiography as the gold standard for initial knee and hand&#x000a0;osteoarthritis assessment.</p>
        <p>Findings consistent with&#x000a0;osteoarthritis in plain radiography include joint space narrowing, subchondral sclerosis, subchondral cysts, and osteophyte formation. Subchondral erosions can be seen in radiographic films of erosive hand osteoarthritis, usually in the PIP and DIP joints. MRI may be helpful in patients with an uncertain diagnosis to evaluate the surrounding joint structures. MRI findings may include loss of articular cartilage, meniscal tears and degeneration, and bone marrow lesions. Ultrasound is an accessible and fast imaging modality that can assess inflammatory changes, including osteophytes and joint effusions, with more specificity to the peripheral joints.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r61">[61]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r62">[62]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r63">[63]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r64">[64]</xref></p>
        <p>Many other biomarkers are being investigated as novel techniques to diagnose early osteoarthritis, monitor disease progression, or respond to interventions. More data is needed to validate these methods. Biomarkers for osteoarthritis include cartilage oligomeric matrix protein, type II collagen degradation peptide, C-terminal telopeptide of type II collagen, chemokines C-X-C motif chemokine ligand 12 and C-X-C chemokine receptor 2, chemokine ligand 2, matrix metalloproteinase 13 (<italic toggle="yes">MMP13</italic>), tissue inhibitor of metalloproteinases (<italic toggle="yes">TIMP</italic>), C-reactive protein, ghrelin, leptin, and hyaluronic acid.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r65">[65]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r66">[66]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Management Overview</bold>
</p>
        <p>Osteoarthritis management is based on the severity and distribution of the disease. The 2019&#x000a0;American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation guidelines<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>for managing hand, knee, and hip&#x000a0;osteoarthritis are outlined in <bold>Tables 4</bold> and <bold>5</bold>.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r67">[67]</xref>&#x000a0;Evidence-based medicine for each treatment determined recommendations (strong, condition, against).&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Nonmedical interventions, including weight loss and exercise, are very effective and should be initially attempted. Weight loss is associated with symptomatic improvement in knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis and even results in structural modification of the knee joint.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r68">[68]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r69">[69]</xref>&#x000a0;Exercise benefits knee, hip, and hand osteoarthritis, but which exercises are most beneficial is unknown.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r70">[70]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r71">[71]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Bracing, splinting, using a cane, and other assistive devices are additional non-medical treatments that should be considered when appropriate. Oral or topical nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) are first-line therapies if pharmacological options are being considered.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;When prescribing oral NSAIDs, renal, cardiovascular, and hematologic toxicity needs to be considered depending on the patient's medical comorbidities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r73">[73]</xref> They should also be&#x000a0;avoided in patients with an increased risk of developing gastrointestinal complications, including those with a history of peptic ulcer disease, older people, and those using corticosteroids.</p>
        <p>Intraarticular corticosteroid injections should be used sparingly as well. In some studies' results, intraarticular corticosteroids have shown benefits for knee&#x000a0;osteoarthritis and ultrasound-guided hip osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r74">[74]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r75">[75]</xref>&#x000a0;Repeated injections have been shown to accelerate joint damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r76">[76]</xref> Serotonin&#x02013;norepinephrine reuptake inhibitors (eg, duloxetine), muscle relaxants, glucosamine and chondroitin sulfate, visco-supplementation injections, and opioids are other measures available as treatment options for osteoarthritis, but have not proven to be very effective and are only conditionally recommended.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r77">[77]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r78">[78]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Some relatively new treatments have not yet been proven effective, including platelet-rich plasma, stem cell treatments, and cannabinoids. Tricyclic antidepressants have also been tried to help control pain. Significant advances in evaluating cartilage biochemistry, nerve transmission, and inflammatory cytokines also point to newer treatments. Further research is needed to evaluate the efficacy and safety of these agents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r55">[55]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r79">[79]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r80">[80]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r81">[81]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r82">[82]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary Knee and Hip Osteoarthritis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The initial management of primary knee and hip osteoarthritis follows the general guidelines outlined above. Weight loss should be attempted in any patient with a BMI greater than 25kg/m<sup>2</sup>. An exercise plan focusing on muscle strengthening and improving range of motion using low-impact modalities should be attempted. These exercises can be implemented at any point in the disease and can be land-based or aquatic.&#x000a0;There is controversial data regarding the efficacy of&#x000a0;glucosamine and chondroitin sulfate supplementation in reducing pain in patients with knee osteoarthritis. No significant reduction in pain or function was reported with the use of vitamin D supplementation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r83">[83]</xref></p>
        <p>If a pharmacological option is being considered, the lowest effective dose of NSAIDs should be used. Topical diclofenac was found to be equivalent to oral NSAIDs in reducing pain in clinical trials.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r72">[72]</xref>&#x000a0;Topical capsaicin may be considered for knee osteoarthritis, but this often causes burning. Acetaminophen was found to lack significant pain reduction in knee osteoarthritis; this is thought to be due to the lack of anti-inflammatory effects compared to NSAIDs. Tramadol is a weak opioid and is another pharmacological option, but it has limited benefit in relieving chronic pain in osteoarthritis alone or combined with acetaminophen compared to a placebo.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r84">[84]</xref><ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="pmid" xlink:href="32171362">[85]</ext-link><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r85">[86]</xref></p>
        <p>Intraarticular corticosteroid injections can also be considered in moderate to severe pain despite the use of oral analgesics. These injections can provide immediate pain relief that can last&#x000a0;up to 12 weeks. Long-term efficacy has not been proven, and frequent injections should be avoided due to the possible risk of promoting further cartilaginous damage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r75">[75]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r85">[86]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r86">[87]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r87">[88]</xref></p>
        <p>Intraarticular hyaluronic acid injections have conflicting and limited evidence demonstrating small benefits in reducing pain and improving knee osteoarthritis function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r78">[78]</xref> These injections remain a treatment option with rare&#x000a0;adverse effects.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r87">[88]</xref>&#x000a0;Treatment with platelet-rich plasma (PRP) injections was found to be superior to intraarticular hyaluronic acid injections.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r88">[89]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r89">[90]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r90">[91]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Intraarticular hyaluronic acid, PRP, and stem cell injections are all recommended against the 2019 American&#x000a0;College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation guidelines, given the lack of supportive data.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r67">[67]</xref>&#x000a0;A genicular nerve block can be used to relieve the pain from knee osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r91">[92]</xref>&#x000a0;Anti-nerve growth factor monoclonal antibodies, such as tanexumab and fasiniumab, have been shown in clinical trials to reduce the pain associated with knee and hip&#x000a0;osteoarthritis but are associated with the acceleration of the disease.</p>
        <p>Partial or total knee replacement is reserved for patients who fail pharmacological and nonpharmacological treatment modalities. Less invasive joint-preserving surgical interventions such as joint resurfacing, unicompartmental knee arthroplasty, or knee or hip osteotomy should be considered in younger patients with limited disease who remain symptomatic despite conservative management. Arthroscopic debridement was found to be ineffective and&#x000a0;may accelerate osteoarthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r92">[93]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary Hand Osteoarthritis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nonpharmacological treatment options in primary hand osteoarthritis include the local application of heat or cold, splinting, and physical and occupational therapy to improve the range of motion and muscle strength. Topical or oral NSAIDs are first-line pharmacological therapy in hand OA. Intraarticular corticosteroid injections are not recommended but are occasionally used for flares. Surgery is often reserved for severe erosive disease with loss of function and persistent pain (see <bold>Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Osteoarthritis, Hand).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r93">[94]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r94">[95]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r95">[96]</xref></p>
