<italic toggle="yes">Bisphosphonates, glucosamine,&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; hydroxychloroquine (HCQ), methotrexate (MTX), tumor necrosis factor inhibitors (TNFi), and interleukin-1 inhibitors (IL-1i)</italic>
</td>
                <td style="width: 290.219px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Bisphosphonates, glucosamine,&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; HCQ, MTX, TNFi, IL-1i</italic>
</td>
                <td style="width: 280.781px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Bisphosphonates, glucosamine,&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; HCQ, MTX, TNFi, IL-1i</italic>
</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 13.2859px;">
                <td style="width: 271px; height: 13.2859px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Platelet-rich plasma injections&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; stem cell injection</italic>
</td>
                <td style="width: 290.219px; height: 13.2859px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Platelet-rich plasma injections&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; stem cell injection</italic>
</td>
                <td style="width: 280.781px; height: 13.2859px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">Platelet-rich plasma injections&#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; &#x000a0; stem cell injection</italic>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>*<bold>Strongly Recommended</bold>,&#x000a0;<bold>Conditionally Recommended</bold>,&#x000a0;Recommended Against&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r67">[67]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of osteoarthritis includes the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Rheumatoid arthritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gout</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Calcium pyrophosphate dihydrate</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemochromatosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fibromyalgia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ankylosing spondylitis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Psoriatic arthritis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neuropathic arthropathy</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Findings that indicate a poor prognostic value require high-quality data currently unavailable. There is a general understanding that certain presentations are linked with a less favorable prognosis, including severe pain, muscle weakness, high BMI, worsening radiographic findings, depression, and widespread disease. A high BMI and intensive physical activity at work predict knee replacement surgery in the future.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r96">[97]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of osteoarthritis include the following:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic pain</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Long-term analgesic use</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reduced joint mobility</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased stability and increased fall risk</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Joint malalignment</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Deformity</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Stress fractures</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemarthrosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Osteonecrosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Joint infection</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Gout and Pseudogout flares</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Depression</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients with osteoarthritis or those at risk for developing osteoarthritis should be educated on lifestyle modifications to reduce the risk of disease progression or development. Patient education should focus on weight loss and moderate daily exercise to improve mobility and stability. Patients should be advised to avoid overuse of pain medication and watch for unfavorable adverse effects from prolonged use of medication. Available assistive devices, including canes and splints, should be offered to patients if clinically indicated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Many patients with osteoarthritis experience chronic pain and difficulty&#x000a0;performing daily activities; an interprofessional approach is necessary to improve this large cohort of patients' quality of life. Primary care clinicians, orthopedic surgeons, rheumatologists, specialty-trained nurses, and physical therapists share the responsibility for the care of these patients. Most importantly, patients should be&#x000a0;counseled and educated&#x000a0;about the prognosis and natural history of the disease.</p>
        <p>Patients should be encouraged to implement lifestyle modifications and control modifiable risk factors to prevent disease progression. Referring patients to physiotherapy, orthotics, occupational therapy, or dietitian services is recommended if clinically indicated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r97">[98]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r98">[99]</xref>&#x000a0;Referral to the orthopedic surgeon should not be delayed in patients who fail to respond adequately to pharmacological management. Joint replacement surgeries have high success rates and can effectively alleviate pain and improve joint alignment that lasts 10 to 15 years.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r99">[100]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-26374.r100">[101]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-26374.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
